<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federal Electoral Institute</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a>–<a href="/years/2014">2014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Instituto Federal Electoral</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Federal Electoral Institute (IFE) was the body that organised federal elections in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from 1990 to 2014. Created after the disputed election of 1988 and made independent of the government in 1996, it introduced photo voter cards and impartial administration that made possible the opposition victory of 2000. It was replaced by the National Electoral Institute in 2014.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federal-electoral-institute.jpg" alt="Federal Electoral Institute" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omar Alejandro Covarrubias Neri based on the arms by Francisco Eppens Helguera (1968) and Juan Manuel Gabino Villascán. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Mexico.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Under the Constitution of 1917, federal elections were run by the Junta Empadronadora, local Computing Councils and Electoral Colleges, which left municipal presidents with great control over polling. The Federal Electoral Law of 7 January 1946, the work of President Manuel Ávila Camacho, created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s first formal electoral body, the Federal Electoral Vigilance Commission, a dependent agency of the Secretariat of the Interior led by the Secretary of the Interior. The same law made independent candidacies illegal, giving political parties sole access to elections.</p><p>In 1973 the commission was replaced by the Federal Electoral Commission, whose governing body included representatives of every registered party. The 1977 political reforms of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-lopez-portillo">José López Portillo</a>, who had stood unopposed in 1976, widened that representation and eased the registration of new parties. As opposition parties grew into a serious threat to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institutional-revolutionary-party">Institutional Revolutionary Party</a> at the end of the 1980s, a reform of 1987 introduced proportional representation in the commission's governing body, which gave the PRI a majority of the party representatives and was generally considered regressive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The controversies surrounding the general election of 1988 led to constitutional reforms in 1989 and to the Federal Code of Electoral Institutions and Procedures, passed in August 1990. The IFE was formally established on 11 October 1990, with the federal legislature, the national political parties and ordinary citizens taking part in its composition. A reform of 1996 strengthened its independence and autonomy by separating the executive branch entirely from its membership and functions, and by reserving the vote in all its directive bodies to councillors with no link to any party or state power.</p><p>The IFE had its own legal personnel and assets. Its headquarters were in the Federal District, and a decentralised structure let it work throughout the country. It registered, funded and oversaw the national political parties and redrew congressional districts, using a redistricting algorithm from 1996. Under electoral reforms begun by President Enrique Peña Nieto, it was dissolved on 4 April 2014 and replaced by the National Electoral Institute, which took over all its duties and added oversight of local and state elections.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Electoral Institute” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Electoral reforms from 1977, and the creation of the <b>Federal Electoral Institute</b> in 1990, made autonomous in 1996, opened politics to the National Action Party (PAN) and the Party of the Democratic Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
